#edb/ref 17592186092331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050612 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050612 #edb/ref 1031 "The Bivariate Normal Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050612 #edb/ref 1033 918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050612 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "58cd99c7-ea79-5204-8fe5-0ad9cfae548a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187020 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187020 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187020 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186547464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187020 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4804197d-04ff-5c8c-8cdf-466d734495d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187038 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187038 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187038 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186547337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187038 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f13c53ef-1e0a-55f0-86d4-e8d2d1425533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188063 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10433" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188063 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/21/21780b1ef6e634615275f2ae4681ac6722398e7400a02a3cb9daf07b8b407da8.svg)\n\nThe covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ for some random variables $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ is given above. Find $a + b + c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188063 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c72f3cc2-5851-5c6b-8edc-74377b88055a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188063 #edb/ref 1074 "For random variables $X_1, \\ldots, X_n,$ the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n![](images/4a/4a9170c03d62e3946d5c825c9baa688fcbcb35247ff123764de3970635977a8d.svg)\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is $\\Sigma_{ii} = \\textrm{Var}[X_i],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i \\neq j$ is $\\Sigma_{ij} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_i, X_j].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a = \\Sigma_{21} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_1] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2] = \\Sigma_{12} = 2$\n\n- $b = \\Sigma_{23} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_3] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_3,X_2] = \\Sigma_{32} = 1$\n\n- $c = \\Sigma_{31} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_3,X_1] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_3] = \\Sigma_{13} = 0$\n\nTherefore, $a + b + c = 2 + 1 +0 = 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188070 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188070 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nGiven that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 3$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.2,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188070 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b315157-43c6-5b8e-ac70-8b781b41cec9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188070 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n![](images/c4/c457df4f5450340a178e0f8c951ece5643acb78295bd98fe935d0a81933a3112.svg)\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2,\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\textrm{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support $S_X,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =10-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support $S_Y,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =5.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =5.8-{2.2}^{2} \\\\  & =0.96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\textrm{E}[XY]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(1)f(2,1)+(4)(1)f(4,1)+(2)(3)f(2,3)+(4)(3)f(4,3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.3+4\\cdot 0.1+6\\cdot 0.2+12\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =7-3\\cdot 2.2 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n![](images/27/27afb746d2ec171983cfa7b06d5f4f2c00909a92ac44abc5890b67d84f6eb5e9.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188071 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188071 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\textrm{E}[X] = 4,$ $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 3,$ mixed moment $\\textrm{E}[XY] = 9,$ and second raw moments $\\textrm{E}[X^2] = 18$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y^2] = 14.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33336c58-c83a-512f-8a79-9223d1442763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188071 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n![](images/4a/4a6aa6958f9b91163fba81f9a29f32d245af5902be44313efc786a2bf4da6099.svg)\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2,\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\textrm{Var}[X]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =18-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =14-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =9-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n![](images/b9/b9d86366c14e62d805d7e046f27ef40cc572048ec7e9f868b6cf31cea94ee7a4.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188072 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10454" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188072 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/4a/4a0e63105483b697842963df430c5341abef68b83501e9d63d17653b88309c59.svg)\n\nIf $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{7}{12},$ $\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\dfrac{5}{8},$ and $\\textrm{E}[XY]=\\dfrac{17}{48},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a22ccc6-495b-5b89-8b7d-775e6e620fe0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188072 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n![](images/e7/e7e614e58780e1600532e0cd172674c921feaae0ff35b47a01d0befaa66230ac.svg)\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2,\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{Var}[X].$\nWe compute $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}y+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\textrm{E}[Y^2]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{4})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x{y}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}+\\frac{3}{10}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{15}-{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{960}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{48}-(\\frac{7}{12})\\cdot (\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{96}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n![](images/a1/a16a9b39d5ff75935e7394d1ff0f3eb03437bc000038b20925d1f91985386b48.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188355 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188355 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Elements of a Covariance Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188355 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188355 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "37d7e5c8-9b77-5eb1-beb8-4355a5a9d625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188362 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188362 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref